<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Ayyubid Emirate of Baalbek</h1><p class="st-meta">1179 CE – 1260 CE (approx.) · capital Baalbek</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Ayyubid principality in the Beqaa Valley, held for half a century by the poet-prince al-Amjad Bahramshah.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalbek">Baalbek</a> in 1174 and granted it in 1179 to his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farrukh-shah">Farrukh Shah</a>. On Farrukh Shah's death in 1182 the town passed to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amjad-bahramshah">al-Amjad Bahramshah</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalbek">Baalbek's</a> rulers lived within the great Roman temple complex, which had been turned into a citadel. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amjad-bahramshah">Bahramshah</a> ruled for nearly fifty years as a minor member of the Ayyubid family, depending on the stronger princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, and was known as a poet. The district produced grain and fruit in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beqaa-valley">Beqaa</a> and controlled the road from Damascus to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ashraf-musa-sultan-of-damascus">Al-Ashraf Musa</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalbek">Baalbek</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-amjad-bahramshah">Bahramshah</a> in 1230, and it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/as-salih-ismail">al-Salih Ismail</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> occupied the town in 1260 and damaged the citadel, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut">Ain Jalut</a> it came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1179 CE – 1260 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 3